course: dictionary
lesson: 9464
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word start can be a noun or a verb, and it has several meanings. Look at the words around it to find which one the writer means. «At the start, Ben had 9 cards.» Here the start is the first moment, before anything changed.
explain: As a verb, start means to begin. «Start reading» tells you to begin reading. The clue is the word that comes after start.
explain: Start can also mean to jump a little in surprise. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: The start (noun) is the beginning of something, like the start of a race.
fact: To start (verb) means to begin doing something, like start reading.
fact: To start something means to make it begin or work.
fact: Start can mean to jump a little in surprise.
fact: «For a start» means as the first point.
exercise: «Bubbles start at tiny places like scratches or specks.» What does start mean here? | *To begin to form | To be cleaned away | To jump in surprise | To finish and disappear
exercise: «At the start, Ben had 9 cards.» What does the start mean? | The end of the game | The middle of the game | *The first moment, before anything changed | A sudden jump
exercise: «Please start reading on page ten.» What does start mean? | To jump in surprise | *To begin | To stop | The beginning of a race
test: In «Ella will start school in September», what does start mean? | To jump in surprise | *To begin | The first point | To stop
test: Ben said, «I got a fright and started.» What does started mean? | *To jump a little in surprise | To make an engine work | To begin a race | To finish a game
exam: «To find how many there were at the start, begin at the end and undo each step.» What does the start mean in this sentence? | The best part | A quick jump | The end of the story | *The beginning, before any changes
